INSERT INTO public.project_document (id, project_id, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, archived_by_ancestor_id, "position", version, owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, updated_by_user_id, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (22, 1, NULL, 'T-54', '# T-54 - Final hygiene pass: a deep review and cleanup of code and board

## Goal

Today the board holds this one open row beside seven parked rows, and the repository carries
comment-level debt (dated ruling citations, one production non-null assertion) against a
standard the repo already enforces elsewhere. When this is done: every acceptance criterion of
the row reads true from command output, the typed review''s findings are fixed or ticketed, the
history findings are tabled to the owner in a comment that never repeats a sensitive string,
`yarn validate` is green on uat, and the committed board snapshot carries the board fully
closed.

## Decisions taken

- **D54.1 Comment standard, repo-wide (2026-10-20).** Comments explain WHY only. A ruling
  citation keeps the ruling''s substance and loses the date anchor; a comment that narrates
  history with no surviving WHY is deleted. In test files, a date that states the frozen
  clock''s meaning (which day, which UTC offset) is the scenario''s definition and survives.
  Precedent: commit 189220dc killed a comment for "styling value plus provenance, both banned".
  Data, not comments, is out of scope: mock seed dates (`mocks/full.ts` and friends),
  production docstring example dates (the `// Returns: ...` blocks in `shared/prayer.ts`
  and `stores/schedule.ts`), and the two "Owner-ruled" note fields in
  `shared/i18n/sourced-names.json` all keep their dates - they are payload or examples of
  output, and changing them risks behaviour.
- **D54.2 The ai/ research tree stays (2026-10-20).** Its TODO mentions quote upstream library
  code under study for parked rows T-42 and T-25; they describe third-party code, not our work,
  and deleting them would gut the parked rows'' records.
- **D54.3 History hits are tabled, never rewritten (2026-10-20).** Rewriting history is the
  owner''s alone. The tabling comment names classes, counts and file families, and never
  repeats a sensitive string, because the board snapshot is committed to the public repo.
- **D54.4 Media ruling (2026-10-20).** The two images in
  `ai/features/global-prayer-times/data/countries/` (and the PDF beside them) get a vision
  read confirming they are third-party website captures or printed material serving parked
  rows'' evidence, not personal or family media; they stay while the parked rows stand. The 51
  other tracked images (widget renders, marketing screenshots, icons, decorations) are app
  renders and public store assets, outside the personal-media class by construction.
- **D54.5 A finding not small and lawful becomes a new Backlog row (2026-10-20).** Nothing
  user-facing changes in place: no pixel, no behaviour, no visual. Any row this creates is
  named in the closing comment, and the Proof below scopes its empty-column claim to rows
  existing at the run''s start.
- **D54.6 Vendor material under .agents/skills/ is third-party (2026-10-20).** Its example
  paths (/Users/dev, /Users/expo, /Users/gabe) stay.
- **D54.7 Version arithmetic (2026-10-20).** Every commit takes the next free patch number,
  fetch origin first. Expected: lane commits 2.1.29 (step 1) and 2.1.30 (step 2); the merge
  commit on uat takes the next free number above every existing commit; the snapshot commit
  takes the one after that. `android/` is gitignored (`.gitignore` line 14), so the
  main-tree `android/app/build.gradle` versionName is edited on disk only, set to the
  snapshot commit''s number, never committed.
- **D54.8 Last-name bootstrap (2026-10-20).** The working-tree grep needs the owner''s last
  name as a token. It is recovered in memory from the history blob scan Step 1 already runs
  (the token appears inside quotation of a past record), used for the working-tree grep, and
  written nowhere - not the board, not a commit, not a log.

## Files

Read: the whole tree (both sweeps), every git blob (history scan), every open board row.
Changed by step 2, measured with the exact regex in Proof criterion 3:
- 60 dated-narration comment lines in production files (shared 24, widgets 15, components 14,
  hooks 2, mocks 2, stores 2, app 1) and 48 in test files, rewritten or deleted per D54.1.
- `stores/countdown.ts` - the one production non-null assertion (L230), typed or guarded.
- `package.json`, `app.json` - version bumps, every commit.
- `android/app/build.gradle` - disk only, in the main tree, per D54.7.
- `board/snapshot/board.sql` - regenerated at the close, in the main tree.
Created: new Backlog rows for findings not fixed in place (D54.5); the history tabling comment
on this row.

## Design

The invariant: every diff line is a comment, a type, dead code, or a record - nothing
user-facing. The typed review starts from `tsc --noEmit` (strict, green at baseline) and
`biome check . --error-on-warnings`: what they leave open is the sweep''s frontier - the
single non-null assertion, double casts, unused exports, pattern drift between modules read in
full, comment debt per D54.1. The sensitive sweep runs two scans: the working tree (grep set
below) and every blob in history (`git cat-file --batch` over all objects). Working-tree hits
are fixed directly with the repo''s placeholder vocabulary; history hits are counted by class
and tabled (D54.3). The board sweep runs the eleven-point Integrity checklist over the seven
parked rows and this one, fixing defects on sight with one comment line each, no moves.

Alternatives rejected: rewriting history ourselves (the owner''s alone); skipping the history
scan because the working tree is clean (the criterion names history); broad refactors of
pattern drift beyond small-and-lawful (ticketed instead, D54.5).

Working-tree grep set (all must print nothing outside .agents/ and .git): home paths
`/Users/[a-z]` beyond the vendor examples (dev, expo, gabe, you, shared, local, literal
`<name>`); the owner''s last name per the D54.8 bootstrap; the Apple team and cert
identifiers recovered the same way; the home-path username outside `capt-muji`; real device UDIDs and
serials (the placeholder names 3T_SERIAL, X8_SERIAL, S23_SERIAL, 8T_SERIAL, IPHONE_UDID,
SIMULATOR_UDID, PHONE_ADDRESS, WIFI_NAME stand in for the real values); keystore, p8, p12,
mobileprovision and service-account files (`git log --all --name-only` on those patterns).

## Steps

1. **Step 1: the sensitive-content sweep, both trees, history tabled** (1 pt, docs) - the
   working-tree grep set proven empty or fixed; history classes counted (home paths, last
   name, Apple IDs, device UDID, bare-rig package ids, scratchpad paths); the vision read of
   the two research images and the PDF; the tabling comment posted on this row.
2. **Step 2: the typed code review and cleanup** (2 pts, code) - tsc/biome/jest-guided sweep of
   `app/`, `components/`, `shared/`, `stores/`, `hooks/`, `modules/`, `widgets/`,
   `device/`, `plugins/`, `api/`, `mocks/`, `scripts/`, `types/`; the 60 production
   and 48 test dated-narration comment lines rewritten or deleted per D54.1; dead code
   deleted; the non-null assertion typed; findings not small-and-lawful ticketed; `yarn
   validate` green.
3. **Step 3: the board sweep and the close** (1 pt, docs) - Integrity checklist across every
   open row with on-sight fixes and one comment line each; merge to uat; the snapshot
   regenerated in the main tree and committed; `yarn validate` green at the close; the row
   closed with its records.

## Risks

- A comment edit lands where biome reformats differently: `yarn validate` shows it; fix with
  `yarn format` before commit.
- A working-tree hit needs a behaviour change to fix: it becomes a Backlog row (D54.5) and the
  closing comment names it.
- The tabling comment repeats a sensitive string: the executor greps the draft against the
  class list before posting; the audit reviewers re-check.
- Version collision with a concurrent session: fetch origin before every bump (house rule).
- The dated-narration rewrite touches a test''s frozen-seed definition by mistake: the D54.1
  test-file rule is the fence, and the jest suite inside `yarn validate` must stay green.

## Proof

- Criterion 1: `list_issues` read at the close shows every non-subtask row that existed at
  the run''s start in Done, Upstream or Deferred, except this row in Done; any row created by
  D54.5 is named in the closing comment with its column.
- Criterion 2: the review record (plan doc v3 or later, or the closing comment) names what was
  read, what was fixed (commit hash), what was ticketed (row number); `tsc --noEmit` exit 0
  inside `yarn validate`.
- Criterion 3: this exact grep over the production dirs
  `grep -rnE ''^\\s*(//|\\*|/\\*).*([Rr]uling|[Oo]wner|[Cc]haos|[Ff]inding|[Aa]udit|[Rr]eport).{0,40}20[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}'' app components shared stores hooks modules widgets device plugins api mocks scripts types`
  prints 0 lines outside `__tests__` and `.test.` files, and the test-file survivors are
  frozen-seed definitions per D54.1; TODO|FIXME over the same dirs prints nothing; the commit
  diff shows no style or behaviour edits.
- Criterion 4: the working-tree grep set prints nothing outside .agents/ and .git; the tabling
  comment carries the history classes with counts.
- Criterion 5: each open row re-read after the sweep, fixed defects carry their one-line
  comment, and the only moves are this row''s own Done move and its step subtasks'' ticks.
- Criterion 6: `yarn validate` exits 0 on the main tree at the final uat commit.

## Grill record

v2 answers the griller''s eight findings: proofs 1-2 rewritten as the start-of-run scoping with
D54.5 rows named (findings 1-2); criterion 3 carries the exact regex and the survivor rulings
in D54.1 (finding 3); subtask ticks allowed in proof 5 (finding 4); the measured 60/48 split
replaces the ~20 estimate and the hedges died (finding 5); D54.8 names the bootstrap (finding
6); D54.7 marks build.gradle disk-only (finding 7); D54.4 rules the remaining media and the
PDF (finding 8).

## Step 1 record (2026-10-20)

Commit 01696725 (2.1.29) on lane/54: the version-bump-only commit, working tree proven clean
against the full grep set (zero true hits for every pattern). History scan across 22,133 blobs:
the four heavy classes (home paths 2,112 lines/647 blobs; last name 9/9; Apple ids 408/256;
device ids 735/373) live solely in objects no ref reaches - never pushed, local object database
only. Reachable history carries two light classes: handle-rooted bare-rig package ids (24
reachable lines in old research records) and /private/tmp scratchpad paths (3 reachable lines).
Tabling comment 2452 on this row carries the classes, counts, families and boundaries, with
zero sensitive strings (self-grepped before posting). Vision read (depth-1 dispatch after the
executor''s depth limit): both research images are third-party computer-set prayer-time tables
(Baku September 2026; ACJU Colombo September), no person or personal scene visible; the PDF is
third-party printed timetable evidence. D54.4 holds, all three stay.

## Step 2 record (2026-10-20)

Commit 465697e1 (2.1.30) on lane/54, 65 files. The dated-narration baseline was 62 production
hits (the plan''s 60 missed two Kotlin sites in modules/) plus 10 wider year-grep stragglers,
and 48 test hits plus 8 stragglers: all stripped of date and provenance, each keeping its
surviving WHY, none deleted whole; frozen-clock definitions in tests kept. Data kept its dates
per D54.1 (mock seeds, docstring examples, the 2024 London verification-corpus line, the two
sourced-names notes). stores/countdown.ts L230: the non-null assertion deleted outright -
clearTimeout accepts undefined (tsc green), the re-entrancy test kills the clearTimeout-deleted
mutant, the guard-deleted mutant is equivalent so the diff stayed at one character, branch
coverage 100 percent. Dead code: none found (the repo''s own unused-export scan agrees, its 13
candidates are deliberate test seams). All five useExhaustiveDependencies disable reasons read
true. Drift dismissed on evidence for store subscription, void fire-and-forget, logger and i18n
access; the 22 undated row/finding/session citations are a consistent repo convention outside
the plan''s named family and stayed for an owner ruling if ever wanted. No Backlog rows needed -
nothing failed the small-and-lawful fence. Final greps: dated family 0, TODO/FIXME 0 across all
production dirs (lane-verified). yarn validate exit 0 at commit time and the pre-commit hook
re-ran the gate.

## Step 3 record, sweep half (2026-10-20)

The eleven-check Integrity sweep ran across all eight open rows. Four defects found and fixed
on sight, one comment line each (2461-2464): the four Upstream titles that carried the column
word (T-239, T-232, T-231, T-51) lost it - the fifth Upstream row T-230 and every other check
passed. The five Upstream rows park by standing law with every PR open and Waiting on naming
its live state; T-42 and T-25 stay parked by the owner''s priority call. Zero relations touch
any open row, so check 11 had nothing to remove. The parked rows'' substance-shape descriptions
(PR link displacing the Goal-first skeleton, a prior-sweep-passed form), null estimates without
steps, and the unset Lane on never-laned Deferred rows all read lawful. No ticket moved. The
close half (merge, snapshot, validate, Done) is the lane''s.

## Audit record (2026-10-20)

Plan-conformance (comment 2468): clean on every check - the fence, the comment sweep, the
sensitive sweep, version law, the board window, the step records. One nice: the gitignored
coverage/ artifact carried the worktree home path on disk; deleted in the fix commit so the
clean-tree claim re-verifies literally.

Blind (comment 2467): two shoulds confirmed and fixed at 65094cd5 (2.1.31) - the duplicated
doc clause in shared/widgetTypes.ts the sweep garbled, and the two dated citations inside JSX
comment blocks (PrayerWidget 521, 902) that the plan''s line-anchored regex could never reach;
their companion test-title citation (widgetContract 499) fixed with them. Two nices: the
sourced-names notes dismissed on D54.1 (data keeps its dates - the reviewer itself read them
defensible), the coverage artifact dismissed into the fix. The proof criterion''s regex gap is
this record''s own note: JSX blocks needed the looser pass, and the residual scan now prints
zero outside the ruled-keep classes (docstring examples, mock seeds, the corpus line).

Both reviews clean after adjudication. yarn validate green at 2.1.31 (227 suites, 5871 passed,
2 skipped, coverage 100 percent); the pre-commit discipline hook verified the bump.', NULL, NULL, '{}', false, false, false, NULL, NULL, 21504, 8, 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', '2026-10-20 00:44:32.909031+00', '2026-10-20 07:20:34.449446+00');
INSERT INTO public.project_document (id, project_id, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, archived_by_ancestor_id, "position", version, owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, updated_by_user_id, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (35, 1, NULL, 'T-327', '# T-327

Re-planned 2026-10-25 on the owner''s run order: the 3T deep-dive is back on, the morning''s deferral is lifted, and the instrument must first be made able to answer the question the first plan died on: compositor truth or encoder loss.

## Goal

The 3T fell below the animation floor it held: the prayer overlay opens janked, the alert and settings sheets drag, while the iPhone XS stays smooth. Done means: the dead zone''s owner is named by measurement (a code regression dated to its commit, or the capture layer, or the device), the 3T''s audited interactions pass the 30fps floor (zero frame gaps over 33ms, 16-17ms cadence the target, 60fps the goal) on Release gate-ON builds if the cause is code, the XS measures unchanged before and after any fix, and `yarn validate` is green.

## The standing record (what the first plan settled)

Steps 1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 of the first plan are done; their records stand in the ticket''s comments. What they established, and this re-plans from:

- Marks never regressed anywhere: overlay_open 134-156, overlay_close 139-158, sheet_alert_open 429-456, sheet_settings_open 368-392, closes 204-210, across every build and env measured.
- The failure is in the frames: tap-adjacent dead zones 176-927ms, and entrance first-draws landing anywhere from 24 to 96 percent of travel run-to-run (the settings sheet sometimes first appears already 96.4 percent traveled).
- The signature is version-invariant (anchors 2.4.11, 2.4.17, 2.4.23, 2.4.24 all show it to varying degree) and env-invariant (mock AND production builds). No commit in 887d7949..1c22014c owns it; a build does not have one signature - the same build snaps and renders cleanly within one night under fixed controls (Step 4''s A/B/A).
- Against the 2026-09-11/12 baselines the regression is real on this same phone. Two candidates survive: a regression predating 2.4.11, or the capture layer (the SD820 encoder) degrading since September. Tonight''s instrument cannot separate them: the video path is the encoder''s word about the compositor.
- Harness debt, all recorded in Steps 1-5: maestro 2.10.0 no longer substitutes `${APP_ID}` from the environment, so the committed flows through baseline-compare.sh launch no app; the committed flows'' tap points are stale on the 3T layout (live tree: row and bell at y~1012, settings 539,1833); a second Athan-family package on the 3T pollutes ReactNativeJS logcat captures; the stock 3s pre-tap close wait is unusable on mock (the overlay auto-closes inside it when the seeded boundary is near).

## Decisions

Standing from the first plan, unchanged: D2 (frame evidence is the verdict, marks are triage; the SF branch''s PASS line is a labelled fallback, never a verdict alone), D5 (the fix preserves the pinned solid-ink invariant; no text renders through opacity), D6 (measured builds share one env so deltas are valid; absolute numbers never compare across envs), D7 (version bump on every commit, fetch first, patch by default).

New, 2026-10-25:

- D9, the instrument first. No further attribution runs until one tap yields both reads in one evidence dir: the encoder read (screenrecord video, per-frame pts) and the compositor read (SurfaceFlinger present timestamps for the app''s layer, resolved from `dumpsys SurfaceFlinger --list` because layer names rotate per relaunch). A dead zone that appears in both is compositor truth; one that appears in video alone is encoder loss. This is Step 9, a harness change with its own commit, validated on glass in Step 10.
- D10, the anchor. 6478619b (1.25.0), the commit the baselines'' own SF note names as measured 2026-09-12 on this 3T. Measured interleaved A/B/A (anchor, tip, anchor) on one night - Step 4''s lesson that sequential runs leave the order confound open. If the era''s tree will not build today, the earliest buildable commit forward from it, named in the record.
- D11, mock-window discipline, binding on every mock-env capture: the 1s pre-tap close variant (the stock 3s pre-wait captures re-opens when the seeded boundary is near), evening seeds read as boundary-adjacent, the second Athan-family package force-stopped before every capture, force-stop and relaunch between flows, battery and charge state recorded with every run. Floor verdicts stay video-plus-vision per D2, with the SF record printed beside them from Step 10 on.
- D12, builds and installs. Measured builds are the harness ritual (e2e/README.md''s build block) in a worktree at the target commit: com.example.athan, Release gate-ON, EXPO_PUBLIC_PERF_MONITOR=1, mock env - the only Android build path that emits marks (shared/perf.ts folds marks out at EXPO_PUBLIC_ENV=prod, and build-mock.zsh unsets EXPO_PUBLIC_* wholesale). Per the owner''s both-platforms standing order (2026-10-25), every build session compiles BOTH platforms in parallel for the same commit: the Android harness ritual under Gradle and the iOS Release build per the XS atlas''s xcodebuild row under Xcode - different toolchains, so they run beside each other inside the one slot session. The tip''s and the fix''s iOS artifacts feed Steps 2 and 15; an anchor or bisect candidate''s iOS artifact is the era''s buildability canary, recorded and discarded, and if an era''s iOS build breaks on today''s toolchain the session notes it and Android proceeds - a September-era iOS breakage says nothing about the current shared code. build-prod.zsh and build-mock.zsh serve owner-line installs only; no step of this re-plan installs over the owner''s line. Every session serializes through the single build slot: `pgrep -fl ''assembleRelease|xcodebuild''` first, wait until clear, never two of the same toolchain. Before every install: `aapt2 dump badging` - harness installs assert package com.example.athan and the versionName and proceed; any owner-package id stops the install. The fleettest rule keys on phones the owner uses, and the owner uses the 3T: this plan operates under the campaign''s standing precedent (the whole prior record - baselines, flows, marks history, the September floors - rode the com.example.athan parallel copy, which never touches the owner''s <pkg> line), and D15''s park asks the owner to bless that dev-id line explicitly. After every install: the User 0 alone assert.
- D13, the run order (owner, voice, 2026-10-25, this re-plan''s authority): the 3T deep-dive is back on; the morning''s deferral until after the release is lifted. Automation tests on the 1+3T, recorded, every frame seen, FPS read: 60fps the goal, 30fps the floor. The iPhone XS must stay measurably unchanged. The owner approves the close personally.
- D14, T-329 reconciled. It remains Step 2, the XS before-picture, unchanged in content; it must complete before Step 13''s fix lands and is otherwise free to run any time after Step 9.
- D15, the terminal override. When the last step is merged and the audit passes, the ticket parks in Awaiting Owner with Waiting on "the owner''s approval to close (run order 2026-10-25)", every subtask ticked, the worktree and branch deleted. The owner approves every ticket of this run personally.

## Files

Read: e2e/README.md (mandatory before any measurement), e2e/AGENTS.md, e2e/baselines/android-3t.json, e2e/scripts/frame-audit.sh, e2e/scripts/baseline-compare.sh, e2e/flows/overlay-x10.yaml, e2e/flows/sheets-x10.yaml, e2e/device-atlas-oneplus3t.md, e2e/device-atlas-iphone-xs.md (its Athan in-app tap rows, measured 2026-10-24), shared/perf.ts (the marks gate), and at Step 13 the suspects the bisect names (hooks/useAnimation.ts, components/prayer/*, components/overlay/*, components/sheets/parts/*, stores/atoms/overlay.ts).

Changed: e2e/scripts/frame-audit.sh (the dual mode), e2e/flows/overlay-x10.yaml and sheets-x10.yaml (the honest appId and the live tap points), e2e/README.md (the traps learned: the SF-pairing note, the mock-safe pre-tap wait), e2e/baselines/android-3t.json (re-based medians and floors, dated), e2e/device-atlas-iphone-xs.md (tap points written back if the 344 visual pass moved them), the fix files Step 13 names, package.json / app.json / android/app/build.gradle (the version bump per D7, every commit).

## Design

The first plan bisected builds before asking whether the instrument can still see. This re-plan inverts that: the instrument''s honesty is settled first (Steps 9-10), the regression is dated against a baseline-era anchor (Step 11), and only a code-named window earns a bisect (Step 12). The verdict tree Step 11 hands to Step 12:

- SF stalls on the tip, clean on the anchor (same night, interleaved): a real code regression entered 1.25.0..2.4.11 - Step 12 bisects that window.
- SF stalls on both tonight: the September floor passes cannot be reproduced on this phone tonight; the change is the device or its capture pipeline, not the code - Step 12 records the degradation dossier and the question goes to the owner.
- SF clean on both while video shows the dead zones: the encoder lost honesty since September; floors are re-judged on the SF record beside what video still shows, and the felt jank is re-read against compositor truth before any code is blamed.

Hypotheses, unchanged from the first plan, tested only if the bisect names their window: H1 the per-row veiled ink (two useDerivedProgress plus a nested interpolateColor per element per frame), H2 the hold-then-hide timers landing inside entrance frames, H3 per-state colour map lookups on the sheet paths, H4 the sheets'' ink tweens and pre-composited backdrop, H5 anything else the window names; beside them the owner''s floated theory (per-row shaded colours, or a Reanimated upgrade due). Fix candidates, chosen only after the cause is named, each preserving D5 and the visual settlement: one veil progress per row shared by its ink styles; hoisted or pre-parsed colour inputs; memoised per-state colour resolution off the render path; the display:none drop batched after every fade settles.

Alternatives rejected: bisecting before the instrument is separated (the first plan''s anchor bisect already produced an attribution the A/B/A dissolved); trusting the video path alone (it is the encoder''s word, and the encoder is a suspect); reverting the veil to opacity fades (violates D5); measuring on the emulator (banned; every perf number names a physical device); prod builds for the marks instrument (marks fold out at ENV=prod).

## Steps

The first plan''s steps 1-8 keep their numbers and records (1, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 done; 2 open). The re-plan''s new steps run as 9-16. Points: 2 code, 3 device; a device step runs alone in a fresh session; e2e/README.md is re-read before every measurement step and the 3T atlas before any 3T screen work.

2. Step 2: the XS before-picture (T-329, 3 points, device). The uat tip as an iOS Release build with EXPO_PUBLIC_PERF_MONITOR=1 per e2e/device-atlas-iphone-xs.md (mock env keeps marks; deltas valid across builds sharing it). Install, discard the first post-install launch. Verify the atlas''s Athan in-app tap rows (measured 2026-10-24, before the 344 visual pass) from a screenshot before driving; write back any that moved. Drive ten iterations of each of the four interactions (overlay open, overlay close, alert sheet, settings sheet) through agent-device; capture PERF_MEASURE over pymobiledevice3 syslog live (tag Athan{React}); two capture runs establish each mark''s spread. Produces: the four before-medians and their band - the line "unchanged" is judged against in Step 15 - plus any atlas rows written back. The owner''s eyeball rides the same build.
9. Step 9: the instrument separated - compositor beside encoder (2 points, code). First deliverable: frame-audit.sh regains the overlay-close pre-tap open mode - committed once at 4ffd1f34 on the first lane''s unmerged branch, absent from uat, so it is recovered from that commit or re-implemented, and it becomes the home of the parameterised pre-tap settle wait. Then the dual run: the video capture as today, plus the SurfaceFlinger latency read of the app''s layer into the same evidence dir, both aligned to the same tap (the layer resolved from `dumpsys SurfaceFlinger --list`; the dump lands after the recorded animation - tap, animation, then dump - so the ring''s last ~127 presents cover the same entrance the video recorded; an idle layer presents nothing, so a dump before the tap reads NO DATA). The analysis prints the two gap lists side by side and the separation verdict per capture; the SF block keeps its NO DATA and exit-status semantics. Same commit fixes the recorded harness debt: the two committed flows carry the literal dev package id (maestro 2.10.0 no longer substitutes `${APP_ID}`) and the live-tree tap points (rows and bell y~1012, settings 539,1833 - re-verified from the live tree at drive time, the atlas discipline); The pre-tap settle wait parameter carries the mock-safe default, recorded in the README''s Gotchas. Shell changes keep the e2e fail-loud style; the e2e scripts carry no jest suite, so Step 10''s on-glass run is the validation. yarn validate green, version bumped. Produces: the dual instrument, committed.
10. Step 10: the dual-instrument verdict on the tip (3 points, device). Build the uat tip harness ritual per D12, install with the badging and User 0 asserts, discard the first launch, verify marks flow. Dual-audit the four interactions (overlay-open, overlay-close through the pre-tap open mode with the mock-safe wait, sheet-alert-open, sheet-settings-open), n>=2 runs each - Step 4 proved one run is one sample. Marks through the now-honest committed flows via baseline-compare.sh. Discipline per D11 throughout. Produces: per interaction, whether the compositor read corroborates the video dead zone - the instrument validated (closing Step 9''s validation) and the tip''s separated verdict.
11. Step 11: the baseline-era anchor dated (3 points, device). Worktree at 6478619b (1.25.0), the era''s own build ritual (its yarn.lock governs; its e2e/README.md is the ritual of record), same asserts (`adb install -d` if the era''s versionCode demands it), first launch discarded. Interleaved A/B/A against the tip: anchor, tip re-run, anchor, one night, battery recorded per run. Produces: the dead zone dated - the code window named, or the device/capture degradation recorded, per the verdict tree.
12. Step 12: the widened bisect or the degradation record (3 points, device). If Step 11 names 1.25.0..2.4.11: halve, build, dual-measure candidates (n>=2; the entrance-render verdict and the SF dead-zone verdict), narrow to the commit that turns the compositor dead zone on; each halving batch is its own device session if the window demands it, and the step''s acceptance is the commit named or the window narrowed to single-digit commits with the measured table. If Step 11 records degradation instead: the dossier (idle-cpu past the mock window, thermal and battery state, the XS control''s instrument independence noted) and the question prepared for the owner. Produces: the regressing commit(s) by measurement, or the degradation dossier.
13. Step 13: the root cause named and the fix implemented (2 points, code). Name the cause with frame evidence: what work entered the tap path or the animation frame, on which thread (ReactNativeJS versus native in the logcat; the SF timeline beside the video gaps), from which commit (read against H1-H5 and the owner''s theory). Implement under D5 and the visual settlement, red-first coverage on every line touched, a mutation pass on guarded logic, yarn validate green. If Step 12 produced the dossier instead: no fix; the record stands and the ticket moves to audit and then Awaiting Owner per D15. Produces: the fix commits on the lane branch, or the owner question.
14. Step 14: post-fix proof on the 3T and the baselines refreshed (3 points, device). Harness Release build of the fix; the four dual audits; floors per D2 - video gaps over 33ms NONE, vision-read contact sheets monotonic, 16-17ms cadence the target, 60fps the goal - with the SF record printed beside. Marks inside the band. Re-base e2e/baselines/android-3t.json medians and floors with a dated note; overlay_close_steady''s UNPROVEN verdict gets its corrected video-path run here. Produces: the passing audits, the refreshed baselines.
15. Step 15: the XS after-picture (3 points, device). The same fix build as an iOS Release with marks, the same drive as Step 2, both capture runs, the atlas''s points, first launch discarded. Produces: the four after-medians inside Step 2''s band, read side by side, and the owner''s eyeball on the same build.
16. Step 16: close-out on the final branch (2 points, code). yarn validate green on the final branch; e2e/README.md''s Gotchas gain the traps the re-plan learned (only ones learned); every commit along the way carries its version bump; the ticket''s records complete; every subtask ticked; then the D15 park: Awaiting Owner, Waiting on "the owner''s approval to close (run order 2026-10-25)".

Points: Steps 2 and 9-16 carry 24; the done steps 1, 3-8 carry 19; the ticket''s estimate is 43.

## Risks

- The dual instrument lies in a new way: SF latency is the compositor''s own accounting and video is the encoder''s; the pairing only helps if both are read on the same tap. The A/B/A and n>=2 discipline carry here too; a single dual capture is a sample, never a verdict.
- The anchor will not build today (SDK drift between the 1.25.0 era and now): take the earliest buildable commit forward, name it in the record; the dating logic survives a small shift.
- The window 1.25.0..2.4.11 is large: the halving loop may span several device sessions; each batch lands its narrowed window in the ticket so the next session resumes cold.
- The degradation branch lands: no fix exists to prove; Steps 14-15 collapse into the owner question and the ticket parks per D15 with the dossier attached. The plan says so now so the collapse is a ruling, not a failure.
- The fix trades cost for a snap: the first-frame-settled rule and the 150/200ms feel are pinned by suites, and the vision read catches a snap in Step 14''s contact sheets.
- The XS band is too loose to prove anything: two before-runs establish the spread; a third capture beats a widened claim.
- Two builds at once: D12''s slot rule; a queued executor waits, a conflict is a stop-and-report.

## Proof

- Acceptance 1 (the regressed marks and the commits identified by measurement or bisect): the standing record closes the marks half (none regressed); Step 12''s per-candidate tables name the commit, or Step 11/12''s record names the non-code owner of the dead zone.
- Acceptance 2 (the root cause named with frame evidence): Step 13''s record citing the dual audit''s paired gap lists, the thread the work landed on, and the named commit''s diff - or the degradation dossier, which names the cause with the same evidence.
- Acceptance 3 and 4 (the overlay and both sheets pass the 30fps floor on a Release gate-ON build): Step 14''s video-path audits printing gaps over 33ms NONE, each contact sheet vision-read, the SF record beside.
- Acceptance 5 (the XS measurably unchanged): Step 15''s four after-medians against Step 2''s recorded band.
- Acceptance 6 (yarn validate green): Steps 13, 14''s build and 16''s final branch.

## Grill findings answered

- 2026-10-25, finding 1 (the pre-tap open mode is not on uat; Steps 10 and 14 would have no overlay-close instrument): fixed. Step 9''s first deliverable is the mode''s recovery or re-implementation from 4ffd1f34, named as its own deliverable.
- 2026-10-25, finding 2 (D12 narrowed the fleettest gate the repo keys on the phone, not the package): fixed. D12 now states the tension openly, operates under the campaign''s standing dev-id precedent with the badging stop guarding the owner''s line, and D15''s park asks the owner to bless the line explicitly.
- 2026-10-25, finding 3 (Android-only measured builds contradict the owner''s both-platforms order of the same day): fixed. Every build session compiles both platforms in parallel for the same commit; the tip''s and fix''s iOS artifacts feed the XS steps, anchor artifacts are recorded-and-discarded canaries with a toolchain-drift note rule.
- 2026-10-25, finding 4 (the SF dump as written lands before the tap and reads NO DATA): fixed. Step 9 now pins the order: tap, animation, then dump, so the ring''s presents cover the entrance the video recorded.
', NULL, NULL, '{}', false, false, false, NULL, NULL, 34816, 7, 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', '2026-10-24 20:02:57.06513+00', '2026-10-25 17:43:31.640077+00');
INSERT INTO public.project_document (id, project_id, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, archived_by_ancestor_id, "position", version, owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, updated_by_user_id, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (24, 1, NULL, 'T-276', '# T-276 — Ten bottom-bar arrangements, screenshotted for the owner''s pick

Owner order 2026-10-20. The deliverable is ten screenshots on this row, not merged code: the branch dies after the shots, uat never moves.

## The three elements (unchanged primitives)

The real SettingsButton (43px wide, the real colors, border, shadow, haptics) and the two real 6px schedule dots (Standard/Extra, active/inactive opacity). Every design rearranges only their container. Real mock data behind them (mock build: prayers sit either side of launch).

## The ten designs

1. **One Line (the owner''s fixed spec)** — the button''s bottom edge sits on the line the dots'' bottoms sit on today, clear of the home indicator; the dots flank it left and right, all three vertically centred on that one line: small dot, big button, small dot.
2. **Corner Pair Left** — button bottom-left corner, dots side-by-side bottom-right. Mirrored asymmetry; frees the whole bottom centre. Trade: the dots sit far from the button they orbit.
3. **Corner Pair Right** — the mirror of 2: button bottom-right, dots bottom-left. Same trade, other thumb.
4. **Floating Pill** — one frosted pill centred on the line, holding dot, button, dot inside it. Trade: reads as a single control; the pill adds its own visual weight.
5. **Integrated Capsule** — the button widens into a capsule and the two dots dock inside its left and right ends. Trade: smallest footprint of all; the dots stop reading as pages to some eyes.
6. **Edge Rail Right** — vertical stack pinned to the right edge: button on top, the two dots below it stacked vertically. Trade: the bottom centre is fully clear; dots read top-to-bottom as pages.
7. **Edge Rail Left** — the mirror of 6 on the left edge.
8. **Segmented Track** — a full-width hairline track near the bottom; the button centred on it, one dot at each end. Trade: maximal symmetry; the track anchors the whole screen foot.
9. **Wide Aperture** — button centred on the line, the two dots pushed out near the screen''s side edges at the same height. Trade: the most air; the dots all but vanish until swiped.
10. **Corner Stack** — button bottom-left, the two dots stacked vertically bottom-right. Trade: diagonal balance, the vertical pair doubles as a page ladder; the busiest of the four corner designs.

## Switcher mechanics (debug-only, branch-only)

- Gate: `EXPO_PUBLIC_DESIGN_SWITCHER=1` at build time. Any build without the env renders today''s shipped layout through a byte-identical path: the gate is the default-off branch.
- A debug overlay in the Navigation chrome layer: an invisible full-width tap strip across the top of the screen; each tap advances the design 1 to 10 and wraps. A tiny `Dn/10` badge (top corner, 10px, low opacity) so every screenshot self-identifies from its pixels.
- Revision (device pass): the phone''s tap runner needs an Apple account session this Mac no longer holds, so commit 2.1.35 added a ten-second auto-cycle beside the strip; the badge alone confirms which design each screenshot shows, and the pixel read verified the order.
- No test coverage demanded (throwaway); `yarn validate` stays green so the build works.

## Shot list

- Worktree `$HOME/athan-gitree/worktrees/lane-276`, branch `lane/276` off uat 2.1.33. Author Athan. Never pushed, never merged, deleted at the close.
- No other build may run at once (the Duo lane builds nothing this run; checked before start).
- Mock iOS Release build of the branch with the switcher env, installed on the physical iPhone XS (`IPHONE_UDID`). One throwaway launch after install (What''s New), then per design: tap strip, settle, screenshot, ten times.
- `design-01.png` through `design-10.png` attached to this row in order, one comment per design naming what varies and what it trades. Screenshots never commit to the repository.

## Steps

- Step 1 (code, 2 pts): the switcher and the ten arrangements land on `lane/276`, validate green, version bump per commit.
- Step 2 (device, 3 pts): mock build on the iPhone XS, the ten screenshots attached in order with their comments.

## Audit

Two reviewers on the protocol''s honesty: the shots match the designs, nothing leaked (no screenshots in the repo, no device identifiers outside placeholders), nothing merged. Not code quality of a throwaway.

## Risks

- A dropped tap desynchronises shot order: the badge on every shot makes desync visible from pixels; a mismatched shot is retaken.
- Metro env-blindness: the build runs with a fresh TMPDIR so no stale transform ships without the switcher.
- iOS build time and signing are the long poles; nothing else runs beside them.', NULL, 'compass', '{}', false, false, false, NULL, NULL, 23552, 2, 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', 'DFppJIU20ig1j5gFRYQIe541IkO7Itfp', '2026-10-20 03:54:08.519408+00', '2026-10-20 05:19:14.381037+00');
INSERT INTO public.project_document (id, project_id, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, archived_by_ancestor_id, "position", version, owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, updated_by_user_id, created_at, updated_at) VALUES (23, 1, NULL, 'T-271', '# T-271

## Goal

What is wrong today: on the iPhone Duo simulator (iOS 27.1), open pose, iOS letterboxes the portrait-locked app into a system-scaled centred 469x669 window on the 951x669pt inner display, with pure-black bars either side (row 264''s measured record). True when this is done: the plist declares landscape so iOS grants the spanning window, a native gate returns landscape only for a screen of the Duo inner display''s class, every other screen - the Duo''s closed outer display, every physical iPhone, every iPad, every Android device - stays portrait exactly as today, the one centred schedule draws unchanged inside the existing 500pt max content width, and the spanning window''s real insets are measured against the 2.1.24 clamp.

THE RULING (owner, 2026-10-20, on the row): no landscape in general; the one exception is the Duo''s open inner display, to remove the black letterbox bars. The ruling narrowly lifts the no-Duo-branch ban for the orientation gate alone: the gate may key on the Duo''s inner display, the layout may not branch.

## Decisions taken

- 2026-10-20 The gate is a native AppDelegate override, not expo-screen-orientation''s lockAsync. Apple documents `application(_:supportedInterfaceOrientationsFor:)` as returning "the total set of interface orientations supported by the app", "intersected with the orientations supported by the root view controller", with the plist "as the default interface orientations" when unimplemented (developer.apple.com, UIApplicationDelegate). Expo SDK 58''s `ExpoAppDelegate` declares that method `open` with the comment "It will use the values from Info.plist as the orientation mask unless a subscriber requested a different orientation" (node_modules/expo/ios/Expo/ExpoAppDelegate.swift:243), so a subclass override in the app''s AppDelegate is the sanctioned seam, synchronous before any rotation commits. Argued twice against the alternative: expo-screen-orientation''s own SDK 58 docs say "On iOS 27 and later, requireFullScreen no longer opts your app out of being resized, so it cannot reliably lock the screen orientation" and "lockAsync may have no effect. The system can refuse the request" (docs.expo.dev/versions/v58.0.0/sdk/screen-orientation) - a lock the system may refuse cannot carry the fence that no physical iPhone ever rotates; and the lock is a JS-initiated async call after launch, so between process start and the lock landing the plist maximum governs unguarded. The package is also not installed, and installing a dependency is an ask-first rule the sleeping owner cannot answer.
- 2026-10-20 app.json keeps orientation "portrait"; the new plugin writes the plist maximum. The iPhone array must carry landscape for iOS to grant the spanning window (row 264''s measured mechanism), but flipping the field to "default" would write `android:screenOrientation="unspecified"` (SDK 58 setAndroidOrientation: `orientation !== ''default'' ? orientation : ''unspecified''`) and let every Android device rotate - a direct fence violation. The plugin sets the iPhone array to all four orientations through withInfoPlist, the exact pattern plugins/portraitOnlyIpad.js already uses to pin the ~ipad array. The owner''s recorded consent to the app.json change this row needs covers the plugins-list entry; the orientation field itself does not change.
- 2026-10-20 The gate keys on the screen class: max(bounds.width, bounds.height) == 951 and min == 669 points, either order. The row''s own measured geometry (951x669 inner, 466x678 closed outer); no shipping iPad panel shares the class (mini 744x1133, Air 820x1180, Pro 11 834x1194, Pro 13 1032x1376). Challenged once: a mode reporting scaled screen bounds makes the key miss and the gate fails closed to portrait, never open. Unknown screen or nil window: the portrait pair.
- 2026-10-20 The gate returns [.portrait, .portraitUpsideDown, .landscapeLeft, .landscapeRight] on the Duo inner display and [.portrait, .portraitUpsideDown] everywhere else - byte-equal to the set a portrait-only plist gives every phone today (SDK 58 Orientation.js maps "portrait" to exactly that pair). Failing closed on every unrecognized screen is the fence.
- 2026-10-20 The 2.1.24 clamp (shared/insets.ts honoredTopInset) is re-verified by measurement; no clamp code changes in this row without a fresh owner order. The spanning window delivers real panel insets, not the phantom ~80 the scaled window delivered; if every open-pose reading (landscape, portrait hold, half-open) delivers a top inset at or below 62, the clamp is the identity there and no code changes. If any open-pose reading exceeds 62 (a real spanning inset the clamp would eat), the row parks in Awaiting Owner with the transcribed reading and the one-line question, and the exemption lands only on the owner''s order. Why, argued twice: the ruling lifts the no-Duo-branch ban for the orientation gate alone, and an inset-honoring branch keyed on the panel would be a second Duo keying in shared code that the ruling does not cover - agent-ruled landing would exceed it; and a panel-class key cannot tell the spanning window from a scaled one on the same panel (row 264''s record has the half-open pose presenting the scaled window with the phantom), so any future exemption must be scoped to the spanning geometry, which only the owner can order with the reading in hand.
- 2026-10-20 No layout change lands. app/Screen.tsx already centres the schedule (maxWidth 500, width 100%, alignSelf center); the wide window only widens the margins. Nothing in JS learns the pose.

