## Design

The Duo''s inner display presents per pose and ignores the app''s orientation preference (D1). The app opts into the edge-to-edge treatment by declaring the four-orientation maximum in the plist; the app delegate then returns the portrait pair for every window, which pins every phone and the closed outer display to portrait - Apple documents the delegate method as the per-window authority intersected at every rotation decision, and row 271 measured phone-class rotation refusals in both directions under exactly this machinery. On the inner display the OS chooses: the tall hold presents the tall canvas carrying the app''s existing portrait layout, and whatever the landscape hold presents is transcribed (D4). No JS learns the pose, no layout value moves, and the closed pose, every phone and every Android device keep today''s behaviour byte-for-byte.

The invariant: on every screen except the Duo''s inner display the app''s effective orientation set equals today''s portrait-only plist exactly; on the inner display nothing is keyed, branched or requested - the OS''s per-pose presentation is the whole of Option B.

Outcome branches, exact:
- Step 1 reads the tall hold spanning edge to edge on today''s build: Option B is already the OS''s presentation of the shipped app. Step 2 and Step 3 each close with a one-line record naming Step 1''s reading, the branch carries no code, and the row proceeds to its audit.
- Step 1 reads the tall hold letterboxed: Step 2 lands the plugin and Step 3 re-measures. A gated build whose tall hold spans tall and whose landscape hold does not present spanning landscape proceeds to the audit. A gated build whose landscape hold presents spanning landscape parks the row in Awaiting Owner per D4 before any Done. A gated build whose tall hold still letterboxes, or presents landscape only, is Apple''s OS refusing the portrait-tall presentation: the row moves to Blocked with Waiting on naming the refusal, the docs'' quotes and the transcribed readings already on the row, and the lane returns BLOCKED with no workaround attempted.

Alternatives rejected:
- Option A''s Duo-keyed landscape gate: cancelled by the owner''s final ruling, never merges.
- requestGeometryUpdate or UIDevice setValue asks: refused or unsupported, and the hack the owner banned.
- expo-screen-orientation lockAsync: the system may refuse it on iOS 27, it lands asynchronously after launch leaving the plist maximum unguarded, and it is a new dependency behind the ask-first rule (row 271''s Decision 1).
- A SceneDelegate insertion re-earning spanning on live unfold: closed impossible by row 271''s Step 2b, both attempts refused by the system (D7).

## Steps

1. Step 1: the tall hold produced and today''s build read in it (device, 3 points). Runs alone in a fresh executor session; row 271 has closed Done with its worktree and branch deleted, so the Duo simulator is this row''s the moment the lane dispatches. Read e2e/README.md''s Gotchas first. Version bump (patch), then build this branch onto the iPhone Duo simulator (iOS 27.1): npx expo run:ios -d SIMULATOR_UDID --configuration Release. Establish the instrument: fold open, then drive orientation through all four values - portrait, portrait-upside-down, landscape-left, landscape-right - transcribing the canvas class the OS reports for each; a value that refuses or no-ops at the tool level falls back to Device Hub''s rotate control before any Blocked call. The tall 669x951 class expected from some rotation value; a landscape-only outcome across all four values and the fallback is itself the reading and names the Blocked branch. Then read today''s build: launched in the tall hold, the window class, the panel-edge pixels against the content bounds (letterbox verdict) and the delivered top and bottom insets; rotated live from the landscape hold into the tall hold under one pid - window class and letterbox verdict; the closed-pose control - portrait, today''s 466x678 class. Screenshots of the tall hold, the landscape-hold control and the closed pose attached to this row from outside the worktree, never committed; SIMULATOR_UDID placeholder in every committed record. One commit: version bump, snapshot, transcribed readings on this row.
2. Step 2: the orientation opt-in lands (code, 2 points). Lands only if Step 1 read the tall hold letterboxed; otherwise it closes with the one-line record naming Step 1''s spanning reading. Read the cancelled gate by hash first (767bb1d9 and 825e832f, Files), once, adapting the pattern without merging any of it. Write plugins/__tests__/duoTallCanvas.test.ts first and watch it fail (red): editAppDelegate inserts the gate into a fixture AppDelegate.swift exactly once (idempotent second run), throws when the marker is absent, leaves ReactNativeDelegate untouched, and the presence sentinel keys on the gate''s own selector inside class AppDelegate (D3''s audit record); the plist edit sets the four orientations; the exported Swift gate carries the literal portrait-pair mask and no panel-class constant; app.json names the plugin and keeps orientation "portrait"; the plugin survives every app.config.ts resolution. Land plugins/duoTallCanvas.js, the app.json entry and the README lines (D8) (green); run the mutation pass on the edit''s guard arms. Version bump first (patch), then prebuild ios in the worktree (npx expo prebuild -p ios) and read the generated ios/Athan/AppDelegate.swift and Info.plist: the gate present inside class AppDelegate, the iPhone array carrying all four, the ~ipad array still portrait-only. yarn validate green. One commit.
3. Step 3: the tall canvas verified on the gated build (device, 3 points). Runs only if Step 2 landed code; otherwise it closes with the one-line record. Alone in a fresh executor session. Version bump (patch), rebuild onto the Duo simulator, then measure: open pose, tall hold - the window spans 669x951, the gradient edge to edge with no pure-black bars, the countdown and schedule centred in the 500pt column rendering top-down across both halves, insets transcribed; open pose, landscape hold - whatever the OS presents, transcribed with its window class; half-open pose - the window class and any letterbox or inset change transcribed (row 271''s grill precedent); closed pose - a rotation attempt refused, portrait, today''s class; an open-closed-open round trip and a rotate round trip, each under one pid; phone-class simulator (iPhone 18 Pro class, iOS 27.0) - rotation attempts in both directions refused. Screenshots of the tall hold and the closed pose attached to this row, never committed. The Blocked branch and the Awaiting Owner park fire here per Design on a refused tall canvas or a spanning landscape hold. One commit.
4. Step 4: the row''s records closed (docs, 1 point). Read the branch diff and record that it names exactly the plugin, its test, app.json, README.md, the version files and board/snapshot/board.sql - or, on the zero-code branch, nothing but the version files and the snapshot, with the README exemption recorded per D8; tick the subtasks; regenerate the snapshot; name the post-merge lane action that hand-sets the gitignored main-tree android/app/build.gradle versionName to the final version, never committed. One commit.

Points: 3 + 2 + 3 + 1 = 9.

## Risks

- The OS presents the inner display landscape only and rotation is refused or ineffective across all four values and the Device Hub fallback: the tall hold never materialises. Shows as Step 1''s instrument readings; the row moves to Blocked with the evidence - the branch the owner pre-ruled.
- The plist maximum letterboxes the tall hold anyway, the compat treatment keyed on fixed orientation rather than pose: Step 3 reads it; Blocked branch.
- The tall canvas proves reachable only through the Duo-keyed all-four mask row 271 carried: Step 3 reads the keying-free gate letterboxing while the keyed shape spanned; Blocked per D4 rather than landing the cancelled keying.
- The landscape hold presents spanning landscape once the plist widens, the rendering the owner cancelled as the Duo''s default posture: the row parks in Awaiting Owner with the reading and screenshot per D4, and the lane returns the question instead of Done.
- A live rotate or unfold keeps the launch-pose rendering until relaunch: row 271''s measured UIKit behaviour, carried as a recorded limitation (D7) and re-verified in Step 3''s round trips.
- An open-pose top inset above 62: the 2.1.24 clamp eats it; the row parks in Awaiting Owner with the transcribed reading per D6, no code change without an owner order.
- A future SDK moves the AppDelegate template: the marker edit throws at prebuild, never silently no-ops, and the unit tests pin the marker against the current template shape; a template-provided supportedInterfaceOrientationsFor would be caught by the selector-keyed sentinel (D3).
- Cross-lane version collisions at merge: uat is read fresh at merge time, the next free version is taken, and any interleaving is recorded in the ticket''s audit comment and allowlisted only when the gate requires it.

## Proof

- The tall canvas renders portrait top-down, edge to edge, no letterbox: Step 1''s or Step 3''s transcribed window class and panel-edge readings on this row, with the attached screenshots.
- The closed pose stays portrait, no other device rotates, phones unchanged: Step 1''s or Step 3''s transcribed refusals and window classes; on the zero-code branch, Step 4''s record that the branch diff names no code; yarn validate green at every step commit.
- Option A never merges: Step 4''s branch-diff record naming no lane/271 content; lane/271 is deleted and this lane restores no ref of it.
