INSERT INTO public.project_document_revision (id, document_id, version, parent_id, title, content, content_json, icon, metadata, full_width, is_private, is_locked, archived_at, "position", owner_user_id, created_by_user_id, created_at) VALUES (63, 23, 1, NULL, 'T-271', '# T-271

## Goal

What is wrong today: on the iPhone Duo simulator (iOS 27.1), open pose, iOS letterboxes the portrait-locked app into a system-scaled centred 469x669 window on the 951x669pt inner display, with pure-black bars either side (row 264''s measured record). True when this is done: the plist declares landscape so iOS grants the spanning window, a native gate returns landscape only for a screen of the Duo inner display''s class, every other screen - the Duo''s closed outer display, every physical iPhone, every iPad, every Android device - stays portrait exactly as today, the one centred schedule draws unchanged inside the existing 500pt max content width, and the spanning window''s real insets are measured against the 2.1.24 clamp.

THE RULING (owner, 2026-10-20, on the row): no landscape in general; the one exception is the Duo''s open inner display, to remove the black letterbox bars. The ruling narrowly lifts the no-Duo-branch ban for the orientation gate alone: the gate may key on the Duo''s inner display, the layout may not branch.

## Decisions taken

- 2026-10-20 The gate is a native AppDelegate override, not expo-screen-orientation''s lockAsync. Apple documents `application(_:supportedInterfaceOrientationsFor:)` as returning "the total set of interface orientations supported by the app", "intersected with the orientations supported by the root view controller", with the plist "as the default interface orientations" when unimplemented (developer.apple.com, UIApplicationDelegate). Expo SDK 58''s `ExpoAppDelegate` declares that method `open` with the comment "It will use the values from Info.plist as the orientation mask unless a subscriber requested a different orientation" (node_modules/expo/ios/Expo/ExpoAppDelegate.swift:243), so a subclass override in the app''s AppDelegate is the sanctioned seam, synchronous before any rotation commits. Argued twice against the alternative: expo-screen-orientation''s own SDK 58 docs say "On iOS 27 and later, requireFullScreen no longer opts your app out of being resized, so it cannot reliably lock the screen orientation" and "lockAsync may have no effect. The system can refuse the request" (docs.expo.dev/versions/v58.0.0/sdk/screen-orientation) - a lock the system may refuse cannot carry the fence that no physical iPhone ever rotates; and the lock is a JS-initiated async call after launch, so between process start and the lock landing the plist maximum governs unguarded. The package is also not installed, and installing a dependency is an ask-first rule the sleeping owner cannot answer.
- 2026-10-20 app.json keeps orientation "portrait"; the new plugin writes the plist maximum. The iPhone array must carry landscape for iOS to grant the spanning window (row 264''s measured mechanism), but flipping the field to "default" would write `android:screenOrientation="unspecified"` (SDK 58 setAndroidOrientation: `orientation !== ''default'' ? orientation : ''unspecified''`) and let every Android device rotate - a direct fence violation. The plugin sets the iPhone array to all four orientations through withInfoPlist, the exact pattern plugins/portraitOnlyIpad.js already uses to pin the ~ipad array. The owner''s recorded consent to the app.json change this row needs covers the plugins-list entry; the orientation field itself does not change.
- 2026-10-20 The gate keys on the screen class: max(bounds.width, bounds.height) == 951 and min == 669 points, either order. The row''s own measured geometry (951x669 inner, 466x678 closed outer); no shipping iPad panel shares the class (mini 744x1133, Air 820x1180, Pro 11 834x1194, Pro 13 1032x1376). Challenged once: a mode reporting scaled screen bounds makes the key miss and the gate fails closed to portrait, never open. Unknown screen or nil window: the portrait pair.
- 2026-10-20 The gate returns [.portrait, .portraitUpsideDown, .landscapeLeft, .landscapeRight] on the Duo inner display and [.portrait, .portraitUpsideDown] everywhere else - byte-equal to the set a portrait-only plist gives every phone today (SDK 58 Orientation.js maps "portrait" to exactly that pair). Failing closed on every unrecognized screen is the fence.
- 2026-10-20 The 2.1.24 clamp (shared/insets.ts honoredTopInset) is re-verified by measurement, with the exemption branch pre-designed. The spanning window delivers real panel insets, not the phantom ~80 the scaled window delivered; if the inner display''s delivered top inset is at or below 62 in both holds the clamp is the identity there and no code changes. If it exceeds 62 in the spanning portrait hold, the clamp would eat a legitimate inset, and the exemption lands in this row red-test-first: on the Duo inner display class (Dimensions screen 951x669, the same class the gate keys on) the delivered top inset is honored at its value. Argued twice: dropping the clamp globally moves phone pixels (the fence forbids), and a window-equals-screen discriminator dies if iOS reports screen dimensions equal to window dimensions in scaled modes, which the row cannot verify cheaply. The panel-class key is the one the ruling lifted for the gate, and it corrects inset truth, not a layout value: no layout branch is added.
- 2026-10-20 No layout change lands. app/Screen.tsx already centres the schedule (maxWidth 500, width 100%, alignSelf center); the wide window only widens the margins. Nothing in JS learns the pose.

## Files

Read (evidence for the design, no change):
- ios/Athan/AppDelegate.swift and ios/Athan/SceneDelegate.swift in the main tree (the prebuild products; AppDelegate subclasses ExpoAppDelegate, and the iOS 27 SDK mandates the scene life cycle through ExpoAppSceneDelegate).
- node_modules/expo/ios/Expo/ExpoAppDelegate.swift (the open supportedInterfaceOrientationsFor at line 243), node_modules/@expo/config-plugins/build/ios/Orientation.js ("portrait" => [Portrait, PortraitUpsideDown]), build/android/Orientation.js ("default" => "unspecified"), build/ios/RequiresFullScreen.js (the iOS 27 resizing note).
- plugins/portraitOnlyIpad.js and plugins/__tests__/replacePreviousNotification.test.ts (the repo plugin pattern: pure edit functions, exported sources, literal-pinning tests).
- app/Screen.tsx (the clamp consumption site), shared/insets.ts, shared/constants.ts (SIZE.contentMaxWidth 500), app/Navigation.tsx, app.config.ts (plugin resolution).
- Plan doc T-264 for the measured geometry, the phantom record and the Vehicle A/B table; e2e/README.md Gotchas before any measurement.

Changed:
- plugins/duoLandscapeGate.js (new): withInfoPlist setting UISupportedInterfaceOrientations to the four-orientation maximum; withAppDelegate inserting the gate into class AppDelegate through an exported pure editAppDelegate that is idempotent and throws when its marker is absent; the Swift gate exported as a source constant.
- plugins/__tests__/duoLandscapeGate.test.ts (new): written red against the absent plugin.
- app.json: "./plugins/duoLandscapeGate" added to plugins directly after ./plugins/portraitOnlyIpad.
- shared/insets.ts, app/Screen.tsx and their tests: only if Step 2''s measured spanning portrait top inset exceeds 62 (the pre-designed exemption branch).
- package.json and app.json version bump (patch) on every commit; board/snapshot/board.sql regenerated per commit. The worktree prebuilds ios only; the main tree''s gitignored android/app/build.gradle is hand-set to the final version after merge, never committed (row 116''s audited precedent).

## Design

The plist carries the maximum; the delegate narrows per screen. iOS reads the iPhone orientation array to grant the spanning window on the Duo''s inner display (row 264: "iOS grants a spanning window only to an app that declares landscape"); at runtime UIKit asks the app delegate for the orientations of the specified window, and the returned mask is intersected with the root view controller''s before any rotation commits. The gate returns the four only when the window''s screen is the Duo inner display class, the portrait pair otherwise, so:

- Duo open, inner display: spanning window; landscape and portrait holds both render edge to edge; the one centred schedule keeps its 500pt column; the black bars are gone because the window is the panel.
- Duo closed, outer display: portrait pair, byte-equal to today.
- Physical iPhone, iPad, every other class: portrait pair, byte-equal to today; the plist maximum is invisible because the delegate mask is the authority at every rotation decision.
- Android: untouched (orientation stays "portrait"; screenOrientation stays "portrait").

The invariant: on every screen except the Duo inner display class the app''s supported set equals today''s portrait-only plist exactly; on that class the window widens and nothing else changes.

Alternatives rejected:
- Vehicle A (landscape in the plist, no gate): unfreezes rotation on every physical iPhone, exactly what the ruling forbids.
- expo-screen-orientation lockAsync: refused by the system on iOS 27+ per its own docs, asynchronous after launch, and a new dependency behind an ask-first rule.
- A root-view-controller gate in the SceneDelegate: wrapping the root VC the factory builds disturbs the tree Expo owns; the delegate override is the seam Expo itself documents, and Apple''s scene-migration page moves only the four life-cycle methods to the scene delegate, leaving the orientation method with the app delegate.

## Steps

1. Step 1: the orientation gate lands (code, 2 points). Write plugins/__tests__/duoLandscapeGate.test.ts first and watch it fail (red): editAppDelegate inserts the gate into a fixture AppDelegate.swift exactly once (idempotent second run), throws when the marker is absent, leaves ReactNativeDelegate untouched; the plist edit sets the four orientations; the exported Swift gate carries the literal masks, the 951 and 669 constants and the portrait-pair fallthrough; app.json names the plugin and keeps orientation "portrait"; the plugin survives every app.config.ts resolution. Land plugins/duoLandscapeGate.js and the app.json entry (green); run the mutation pass on the edit''s guard arms (a mutated marker throws; the already-present selector short-circuits). Prebuild ios in the worktree (npx expo prebuild -p ios) and read the generated ios/Athan/AppDelegate.swift and Info.plist: the gate present inside class AppDelegate, the iPhone array carrying all four, the ~ipad array still portrait-only. yarn validate green. Version bump, snapshot regenerated, one commit.
2. Step 2: the ruled outcome measured on the simulators (device, 3 points). Runs alone in a fresh executor session. Read e2e/README.md''s Gotchas first. Build from the branch: npx expo run:ios -d SIMULATOR_UDID --configuration Release. On the iPhone Duo simulator (iOS 27.1): open pose, landscape - the window spans 951x669, the gradient reads edge to edge with no pure-black side bars, the countdown and schedule centred in the 500pt column; open pose, portrait hold - the 669x951 window class, layout centred, the delivered top and bottom insets transcribed, the clamp verdict recorded (identity, or the exemption branch from Decision 5 landed red-test-first when the reading demands it); closed pose - a rotation attempt refused, the window in today''s 466x678 class, rendering equal to today; an open-closed-open round trip - the process stays live and each pose renders its own geometry. On the phone-class simulator (iPhone 18 Pro class, iOS 27.0): rotation attempts in both directions refuse; the snapshot stays portrait. One spanning-landscape screenshot attached to this row from scratch outside the worktree, never committed; SIMULATOR_UDID placeholder in every committed record. Version bump, snapshot, one commit.
3. Step 3: the row''s records closed (docs, 1 point). Read the branch diff and record that it names exactly the plugin, its test, app.json, the version files and board/snapshot/board.sql, plus the inset files when the exemption branch ran, so no phone-tuned layout value moved; tick the subtasks; regenerate the snapshot. Version bump, snapshot, one commit.

## Risks

- UIKit may not re-ask the mask when the fold closes: the closed pose would keep landscape until the next ask. Shows as the closed pose accepting rotation after the round trip in Step 2. Fix branch: the SceneDelegate observes the scene''s screen change and calls setNeedsUpdateOfSupportedInterfaceOrientations on the root view controller.
- The delegate may be asked with a nil window early in launch: the gate fails closed (portrait pair); the worst case on the open Duo is a portrait presentation until the next ask. Shows as a letterboxed or portrait launch frame in Step 2. Fix branch: key on the first connected UIWindowScene''s screen when the window is nil.
- The spanning portrait top inset may exceed 62: Decision 5''s pre-designed exemption branch.
- A future SDK may change the AppDelegate template: the marker edit throws at prebuild, never silently no-ops, and the unit tests pin the marker against the current template shape.
- Resizable-window contexts (iPhone Mirroring, iPad resizing) treat orientations as a preference on iOS 27+ (Expo SDK 58 docs): outside the verified fence, which covers full-screen phones, the Duo''s poses and full-screen iPads. Recorded, not chased.
- The panel class constant is exact points: a future Duo hardware revision with a different inner panel fails closed to portrait. Recorded, not chased; the constant is named for exactly this read.

## Proof

- Gate named and implemented: Step 1''s prebuild read (the gate in AppDelegate.swift, the four in the iPhone array, ~ipad still portrait-only) and the test suite pinning masks, constants, idempotence and wiring.
- Duo open landscape spans, no bars, centred, transcribed: Step 2''s transcribed readings on this row, plus the attached screenshot.
- Closed pose stays portrait: Step 2''s transcribed refusal and window class on this row.
- No physical iPhone rotates, gate unit-tested: Step 2''s transcribed rotation refusals on the phone-class simulator; the plugin test suite.
- Otherwise unchanged, validate green: Step 3''s recorded branch diff naming no layout value; the phone simulator''s portrait snapshot equal to today''s; yarn validate green at every step commit.
