### Why the compounding disappears

A translucent glyph paints a·ink on each stroke; two overlapping strokes leave 1−(1−a)² ≈ 2a−a² of the ink, so the overlap reads brighter than a single stroke. At muted''s 0.38 the overlap carries 0.62 against 0.38 — 1.6× the single-stroke ink, the band the owner reads. A solid glyph paints alpha 1 on every stroke: overlap equals single stroke, one even ink. This holds mid-animation: a colour tween interpolates opaque colours, so no intermediate frame is translucent.

## Decisions taken

- 2026-10-21 — Gradient dim roles take the gradient-midpoint composite as their one solid (table above). Why: the owner banned adaptive shades; the list sits at the midpoint, the corner deviation is tabled, and the owner''s phone read rules the final word.
- 2026-10-21 — Roles on solid surfaces flatten exactly over their surface; `sheetSubtitle` becomes `#416899` and its five local literals collapse into the constant. The 6% card wash under the Settings and Alert card subtitles shifts the composite by at most 5/255 per channel, under the just-noticeable difference; the bare-sheet flatten serves them all.
- 2026-10-21 — The disabled matrix is carried by state-conditional solid colour maps (the table above), one entry per element per state, encoded as module constants in the components that render them. The wrapper opacities B1-B6 are deleted. Text-free views inside those groups (stepper wash, segment pill, colour-preview dot and border) hold their pixels exactly - the wash and pill by pre-flattening to per-state solids (audit-amended: the flatten is the chosen equivalent, pixels verified identical), the preview dot and border by an equivalent opacity on a text-free subtree.
- 2026-10-21 — A hide that must hold layout paints the surface''s own colour (QiblaSubtitle lines over the sheet) or `transparent` (ColorPicker reset label, Qibla donor). Alpha 0 renders no ink, so nothing compounds; alpha strictly between 0 and 1 stays banned on text colours. The SoundItem countdown becomes a conditional render: the play icon is end-anchored and the countdown sits on its lead side, so its absence moves nothing, and the colour tween lives at row level and survives the remount.
- 2026-10-21 — The separating rule for fades: a fade whose resting state is hidden or dimmed converts to colour; a transition that passes between two settled states (modal and Help entrances) rides view compositing. The three resting-hidden fades — A6 (ago badge), B8 (prayer rows), B9 (info box) — move to Step 3: no solid colour equals invisible over a gradient, so the conversion needs a veil-merge colour the owner reads first.
- 2026-10-21 — `text.secondary` keeps one constant (`#6c79c2`, its gradient habitat: Day location, Countdown caption). The ColorPicker reset label takes its own sheet-flattened solids (`#5b77a4` shown, `#1f3055` bar-hidden) as a named local role, and the sheet grabber indicator (Shared.tsx:55, a view) moves to a dedicated `#5b77a4` view constant so re-valuing the text constant re-inks no view. Fixed per-surface constants do not follow the background; this is not the owner-rejected adaptive shade.

- 2026-10-21 — Step 3, argued once for the prayer rows and the ago badge: the resting-hidden fades convert by ink tween toward ONE ruled veil constant, `COLORS.gradient.overlay.veil` = `#0b0016` — the overlay backdrop composited at the list''s band (the overlay gradient #110022→#000000 read at the band midpoint #080011, plus the veil glow''s band-average wash ≈ 0.02 alpha of #8000ff, which reaches ≈0.09 only on the first row where the glow''s skirt ends) — and each element then drops from the tree after its fade (the OverlayInfoBox hold-then-hide pattern: display flips to none at the fade''s end, back to flex the instant the overlay closes, so the resting frames are exact and the shared value survives). Why ink and not the covering veil: a covering veil must end opaque at rest-open, and no solid equals the live backdrop behind a hidden row (the overlay gradient plus the glow''s skirt) — an opaque veil would paint row-shaped bands of the wrong colour and cut slats out of the glow, so it cannot hold the resting frame the way display:none can; and an ink tween is the ticket''s own thesis (colour carries the dim, the W3C/AOSP prior art''s one redress), it composes with the row''s existing selection tween exactly (the visible ink interpolates inside the veil interpolation, so at veil 0 it is bit-identical to today), and over the veil backdrop it reproduces today''s composite formula for formula wherever the constant meets the local backdrop. The deviation is tabled: the ghost during the 150-200 ms fade reads at most ≈1.1:1 against the band''s ends (first row, where the glow lifts the backdrop to ≈#180030, and the last row and the badge, where it falls to ≈#06000c), below the just-noticeable difference, present only mid-fade, and the resting frames are identical to today''s on both ends.
- 2026-10-21 — Step 3, argued twice for the info box: its layer fade decomposes into a card-face fade and inks that rise from the card''s own surface — the face (background, border, radius) becomes a text-free absolute sibling whose opacity rides the reveal, and each ink (title, explanation) interpolates from `COLORS.surface.elevated` (#12062a) to its final colour on the same progress, while the badge, the separator and the arrows fade as text-free views. Why not the covering veil here: the box floats over the veil backdrop while THAT is still fading in and the rows are still fading out, so a covering veil would have to match a composite that changes under it — impossible for any constant — whereas the card''s own surface is exact and opaque, the plan''s formula-for-formula case (an ink tween toward the surface equals an opacity fade over that surface), and no glyph is ever translucent because the texts never sit inside the fading subtree. The resting frames are identical: closed is the existing display:none, open is the opaque face under final inks, pixel for pixel today''s card.

